# boardid.config
#
# erlinit runs this (-d /usr/bin/boardid) to build the hostname, so it is
# what keeps two watches apart. That matters for more than cosmetics:
# VintageNetDirect's DHCP server picks the usb0 /30 by hashing the hostname
# into 172.31.0.0/16, so two devices answering to the same name also claim
# the same address.

# The Qualcomm SoC serial, exposed by the mainline soc driver. The best
# identifier on this board — but it arrives via SMEM, whose probe can be
# deferred, and erlinit reads the ID early, so the eMMC CID below is the
# one that reliably answers in time.
#
# `-t text` emits the digits as they appear in sysfs; without it the bytes
# are hex-encoded.
-b binfile -f /sys/devices/soc0/serial_number -l 10 -t text

# The eMMC CID, for when the above is not populated yet. This cannot be
# late: the rootfs was just mounted from this device.
#
# Offset 20 skips the manufacturer and product-name fields, which are
# identical across units, and lands on the card's serial number. Reading
# from 0 would give every watch the same first four characters, and the
# hostname pattern only uses four.
-b binfile -f /sys/block/mmcblk0/device/cid -k 20 -l 8 -t text

# Last resort, so a device without either still gets an ID.
-b cpuinfo
